A||||3||wayne||Migrating water
Z||000000||wayne||07-02-99||06:06 AM||||I'm having trouble with my 85 GT. If the car sets for a few days the reservoir for the radiator fills up. Of course this means very little water back in the engine block when it starts up. It starts to overheat within a few miles. I've have the radiator reworked at the radiator shop and tried a new radiator cap with no help. Sometimes this even happens after you shut the car off, water fills the reservoir up. Temp gauge is in the normal range with this happens so it doesn't appear that it overheated.<p>I put a radiator pressure tester on and ran the engine checking for cracked head gasket all seemed to be normal.<p>any suggestions?<p>wayne
Z||000001||Racer 1||07-02-99||03:35 PM||||Are you using 50/50 radiator fluid and water mixture?Remeber you cant run only on water at has a lower bowling point then radiator fluid.Also radiator fluid lubes your water pump inside.And make sure you filling it to the lines on the raditor bottle cold before stating the engine!Also your themostat is probably gone and isnt closing all the way.your engines filler cap is mounted higher then the raidator  itself causing your water problem!!!!
Z||000002||Bruce||07-02-99||06:42 PM||||It's been my experience that the thermostats have a relatively short life in the Fiero configuration. It's about $8 for a new one, and probably a good place to start. Plus, if your thermostat is letting your car run too cool, your $%#^&*.
Z||000003||wayne||07-06-99||10:48 AM||||Thanks for the input.<br>I checked the thermostat and it seemed to be OK this time.<p>In the past it has been out of place. <br>My 85 uses the plug in type termostat. <br>Can I or is it worth while to change over to the standard type that is mounted under the water housing?<p>wayne
